-- title:
--   Values of K^× in C are powers of v(varpi)
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal{O}$ be a discrete valuation ring (a commutative domain), $K$ its fraction field, and $\pi$ an element of $\mathcal{O}$; let $C$ be a field carrying a valuation $v$ with values in a linearly ordered commutative group with zero $\Gamma_0$ and equipped with a $K$-algebra structure, and let $R$ be a commutative ring which is an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra and a subalgebra-like source for $C$, all the algebra maps being compatible as $\mathcal{O}$-algebras over $R$ and over $K$. Let $\varpi$ be a pseudo-uniformiser of $K$ relative to $C$, that is, an element $\varpi.\varpi \in K$ whose image in $C$ satisfies $0 < v(\varpi) < 1$ and such that for every $a \in K^\times$ there is $N \in \mathbb{N}$ with $v(\varpi)^N \le v(a) \le (v(\varpi)^{-1})^N$. Assume `IsAdicFrame π ϖ R`: $\pi$ is irreducible in $\mathcal{O}$; $R \to C$ is injective; the image of $R$ in $C$ is exactly the set $\{c : v(c) \le 1\}$; $R$ is adically complete for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$; an element $a \in K$ satisfies $v(a) \le 1$ in $C$ if and only if $a$ lies in the image of $\mathcal{O} \to K$; the image of $K$ in $C$ is closed; and $\pi$ and $\varpi.\varpi$ have the same image in $C$. Then for every $a \in K$ with $a \neq 0$ there is an integer $k$ with $v(a) = v(\varpi.\varpi)^k$ in $\Gamma_0$, the images being taken in $C$.
--
--   The assertion is that, inside the valuation group of $C$, the values taken on $K^\times$ form the cyclic subgroup generated by $v(\varpi)$, as one expects when $\mathcal{O}$ is the valuation ring of $K$ in $C$ and $\pi$ is a uniformiser. It is used in the verification that adic points of the formal model surject onto the $C$-points of Drinfeld's $p$-adic upper half plane, in [`CerednikDrinfeld.FormalOmega.AdicPoint.toOmega_surjOn`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.FormalOmega.AdicPoint.toOmega_surjOn), where a cover of the space by tubes requires control of $v$ on $K^\times$.
